title: Why going back is the script's business
tags: [[Scripting the GUI]]
kind: rationale
summary: Backspace is reserved by the GUI but undoes nothing itself — it tells the script, which returns the event log to where it was before the answer.
audience: dev


Classifying files is a long run of one-key answers, and a wrong one is noticed one
question too late. Going back was added in September 2026, with two parts that do not
live in the same place.

!! The key is the GUI's

`backspace` joined `escape` and the keys of `command-input:focus` and
`script-keys:toggle` among the reserved combos (`keybindings.rs`, `reserved_combos`):
`POST /gui/input` refuses a wait that asks for it. So no script can take the key for
something else, and it means the same thing in every script — like Escape. But where
Escape ends the ''run'', Backspace ends nothing: the shell resolves the wait with the
value `back` (`inputWaitAction` in `store.svelte.ts`).

!! The meaning is the script's

The GUI does not know what the last answer wrote: one `mf tag`, a write over a whole
subtree, a file moved. So it undoes nothing. `mf_gui_ask_answer` turns `back` into the
letter `u` for a caller that listed `u`, and ''asks again'' for one that did not —
never a silent no-op, and never the default branch of the caller's `case`, which
stops the run.

A script that writes as it goes undoes through the ''event log'', the one exact way:
it notes the operation id before each answer (`mf log head`, added for this) and
returns to it (`mf log rollback --id`). That restores the field rows with their ids
and the versions ([[Rollback]]); an ''untag'' written by hand restores neither, and has
to know every rule the write applied ([[Tag subsumption and exclusivity]]).

Two choices were the user's: the stack goes ''back to the first question'', and going
back ''re-opens the subtree'' the answer had settled — a re-asked question whose
consequences stay in place would be of no use.

The letter was `b` at first, and became `u` — undo — with the rest of the undo
vocabulary ([[Undo and redo]]).

!! The counter trap

A script that keeps counters of its own must push, with each answer, the counters as
they were ''before'' the entry was counted. Taken after, going back subtracts twice and
''N left'' goes negative — found by a manual run, not by the tests. `gui-tag-folder.sh`
no longer has the problem: its counter is a `count` over the query that is its state,
so it is right again as soon as the log is back ([[The query is the walk state]]).
